    </patterns>
  </knowledge>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Verify against live metadata: sprk_todo's regarding lookups for project, matter and work assignment, and the PascalCase @odata.bind navigation property for each. Record all three names and their source in the notes file.</step>
    <step order="1">Read the documents and invoices module registrations plus Tier2ScopeFilterInjector, and decide where the READ half belongs: a registered `todos` external module on the generic scoped-fetch plane, or sibling routes. Write the decision and its reason down BEFORE implementing.</step>
    <step order="2">Implement the read half. Whichever shape, prove a matter-parented to-do is listed for a caller holding that matter and is NOT listed for one who is not -- and that an out-of-scope root denies rather than returning an empty collection.</step>
    <step order="3">Implement the create half: parameterize the specific-lookup bind and the display-name lookup by parent type, reusing ApplyResolverFieldsAsync unchanged. Enforce the one-parent ADR-024 rule so no two-parent row can be created.</step>
    <step order="4">Decide and implement the create-side level question per the constraint (membership-implies-create vs blocked on the levelless roots). Comment the choice at the decision site, not only in notes.</step>
    <step order="5">Extend ExternalTodoScopeTests: for EACH of the three roots, a positive and a negative on both list and create; an ambiguous-parent create is rejected; the resolver fields are stamped with the right entity for a matter parent. Keep the call-count assertions -- a create must not reach Dataverse on a deny.</step>
    <step order="6">Perturb, and record counts for each: (a) revert GetTodosAsync's filter to project-only; (b) bypass the create-side scope check; (c) cross-wire the matter dimension to the work-assignment set; (d) stamp the project entity name into a matter-parented create's resolver fields; (e) allow two parent lookups in one create. A zero-failure perturbation means the test is at the wrong level -- fix the test.</step>
    <step order="7">Amend spec.md: FR-08's acceptance criteria name only the update path. State read/create/update parity across the three roots explicitly, so the asymmetry cannot silently reopen. This is the same lesson as FR-09's missing expiry criterion (task 023).</step>
    <step order="8">Full suite (all SEVEN test projects); publish size; notes file; update TASK-INDEX.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If the matter or work-assignment @odata.bind navigation property cannot be read from $metadata in this environment, STOP and escalate rather than guessing. Guessing a nav prop is the exact hazard task 021 exists to avoid.</trigger>
    <trigger>If the read half turns out to require a change to the shipped external SPA client contract (i.e. the generic scoped-fetch plane cannot serve the To Do view without a client change), STOP and surface it -- coordinating a client change across SPA-r1/r2/r3 is a scope decision, not an implementation detail.</trigger>
    <trigger>If honouring the owner's "any regarding entity" intent turns out to require accessible sets that do not exist (service request -> task 028; the nine other regarding types -> no set at all), do NOT invent them here. Deliver the three roots, and state plainly in the notes which of the owner's stated intent remains unimplemented and what each missing piece needs.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>The read scoping mechanism exists twice (documents module: project + matter + work assignment OR'd; invoices module: two dimensions), and the create-side resolver stamping is already entity-generic in ApplyResolverFieldsAsync. TodoRegardingBuilder already owns the entity-to-lookup mapping on the SDK path.</existing>
    <extension>Extends three existing files by adding a third instance of an established pattern. No new service, no new interface, no second mapping table.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>The plane's write surface is wider than its read surface: a caller may PATCH a matter- or work-assignment-parented to-do that the same plane refuses to list and cannot create. That is an incoherent contract the SPA cannot express, it leaves the owner's stated product intent (parent flows from creation context) unimplemented for two of the three supported roots, and an asymmetry between what a surface can read and what it can write is precisely the shape that hides authorization defects -- FR-08's acceptance criteria never mentioned read or create, which is how this gap was created in the first place.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

  <outputs>
    <output type="code">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/ExternalDataService.cs</output>
    <output type="code">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Api/ExternalAccess/ExternalProjectDataEndpoints.cs</output>
    <output type="code">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/DI/ExternalAccessModule.cs</output>
